<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace TCM\Core;

/** Public, version-stamped learning guides. Keep the subject pages self-contained and beginner friendly. */
final class LearnGuideCatalog
{
    /** @return array<string,array<string,mixed>> */
    public static function all(): array
    {
        $guides = [
            'python' => [
                'name' => 'Python', 'category' => 'Programming language', 'version' => 'Python 3.15.0',
                'versionNote' => 'Latest stable release as of 21 October 2026. Start with 3.15 for new learning; check library compatibility before choosing it for a production project.',
                'level' => 'Beginner to advanced', 'duration' => '15 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-braces-asterisk',
                'summary' => 'Start with your first Python program and progress to clean, tested applications, asynchronous programming and project packaging.',
                'prerequisites' => 'No programming experience is required. Install Python 3.15 and an editor, or use an online Python shell to begin.',
                'outcome' => 'Build command-line tools, automate repetitive work, read and write data, and structure small applications with tests.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Programming Foundations',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. Set up Python and run your first program', 'explanation' => 'Python is a programming language, and the interpreter is the program that reads your code and runs it. Save the instructions in a file ending in .py, then run that file from a terminal with python filename.py. print shows a result so you can check what your program did. Text values are called strings and can be written inside single or double quotes.', 'example' => "print('Hello, world!')\nprint(2 + 3)", 'practice' => 'Print your name and one goal for learning Python. Run the file again after changing the text.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Variables, names and basic types', 'explanation' => 'A variable is a label your program uses to find a value again, much like a name on a storage box. Python recognizes common kinds of values: int means a whole number, float means a number with a decimal, str means text, and bool means True or False. Choose names such as lessons_finished so another person can understand your code. Avoid using names like list because Python already uses them for built-in tools.', 'example' => "learner_name = 'Asha'\nlessons_finished = 3\nprogress = 0.25\nis_enrolled = True\nprint(learner_name, lessons_finished)", 'practice' => 'Create variables for a product name, price and stock count, then print a short summary.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Strings, formatting and input', 'explanation' => 'A string is text, such as a name or a message. You can clean text with strip(), change its letter case with lower(), and replace one part with another. An f-string makes a sentence from text and values by placing each value inside braces. One detail to remember: input() always gives you text, even when someone types digits, so convert that answer to int or float before calculating with it.', 'example' => "name = input('Your name: ').strip()\nage = int(input('Your age: '))\nprint(f'Hi {name}; next year you will be {age + 1}.')", 'practice' => 'Ask for two numbers, convert them to integers, and print their sum and product.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Operators, conditions and branching', 'explanation' => 'Operators are symbols that do work with values: + adds numbers, while == checks whether two values are equal. A comparison produces a yes-or-no result called a Boolean. if, elif and else let a program choose what to do, like a set of rules for deciding a delivery fee. Python uses indentation to show which instructions belong to each choice, so consistent spaces are part of the program’s meaning.', 'example' => "score = 78\nif score >= 90:\n    grade = 'A'\nelif score >= 60:\n    grade = 'Pass'\nelse:\n    grade = 'Retry'\nprint(grade)", 'practice' => 'Write a shipping-cost rule that gives free delivery above a chosen order total.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Loops and iteration', 'explanation' => 'A loop repeats a small set of instructions. Use for to work through items one by one, such as every topic in a list. Use while when the program should keep going until something changes, such as asking again until the user enters a valid answer. break ends a loop early; continue skips the current item. enumerate is useful when you want both an item and its position, like numbering a list of lessons.', 'example' => "topics = ['types', 'loops', 'functions']\nfor number, topic in enumerate(topics, start=1):\n    print(f'{number}. {topic}')", 'practice' => 'Loop over 1 through 20 and print only the numbers divisible by three.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Lists, tuples, dictionaries and sets', 'explanation' => 'Choose a collection based on how you use the data: lists are ordered and mutable, tuples are ordered and usually fixed, dictionaries map unique keys to values, and sets store unique members. Membership checks such as item in collection make intent clear.', 'example' => "course = {'name': 'Python', 'modules': 12}\nmodules = ['types', 'loops', 'functions']\ncompleted = {'types'}\ncourse['modules'] = len(modules)\nprint('loops' in modules, completed)", 'practice' => 'Model a small library book using a dictionary and keep a list of three titles.'],
                    ['title' => '7. Functions, parameters and scope', 'explanation' => 'Functions name a reusable unit of work. Pass data through parameters and return results instead of relying on hidden global state. Default values are evaluated once, so avoid mutable defaults such as [] and use None when a fresh list is needed.', 'example' => "def calculate_total(prices, tax_rate=0.0):\n    subtotal = sum(prices)\n    return subtotal * (1 + tax_rate)\n\nprint(calculate_total([100, 50], tax_rate=0.18))", 'practice' => 'Write a function that accepts a list of scores and returns the average; decide what it should do for an empty list.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Modules, packages and virtual environments', 'explanation' => 'A module is a Python file; a package groups modules. Import only what you need and keep application code separate from entry-point code. A virtual environment gives one project isolated dependencies. Record required packages so another developer can reproduce the environment.', 'example' => "python -m venv .venv\n# Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\n# macOS/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install requests\npython -m pip freeze > requirements.txt", 'practice' => 'Create a project folder with app.py and a helper module. Import one function from the helper and run it.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Errors, exceptions and defensive code', 'explanation' => 'An exception signals that an operation could not complete. Catch only errors you can handle, keep the try block small and give the user a useful message. Use finally for cleanup that must run, and raise a clear exception when a function receives invalid input.', 'example' => "def read_count(text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except ValueError as error:\n        raise ValueError('Count must be a whole number.') from error\n\nprint(read_count('12'))", 'practice' => 'Make a safe integer-input helper and show a helpful message instead of a traceback for invalid input.'],
                    ['title' => '10. Files, paths and structured data', 'explanation' => 'Use pathlib for paths and open files with a with block so they close even if an error occurs. Choose an explicit text encoding. JSON is useful for simple structured data; validate external data because a valid JSON document may still have the wrong shape.', 'example' => "import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndata = {'name': 'Asha', 'done': ['types']}\npath = Path('progress.json')\npath.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2), encoding='utf-8')\nloaded = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\nprint(loaded['name'])", 'practice' => 'Save a small task list as JSON, then load it and print only incomplete tasks.'],
                    ['title' => '11. Classes, objects and composition', 'explanation' => 'A class defines how objects store state and provide behavior. __init__ sets up a new instance; self refers to that instance. Use composition when one object can work with another, and introduce inheritance only for a genuine “is a” relationship. Keep classes focused on one clear responsibility.', 'example' => "class Timer:\n    def __init__(self, label):\n        self.label = label\n        self.seconds = 0\n\n    def add(self, seconds):\n        if seconds < 0:\n            raise ValueError('seconds must be non-negative')\n        self.seconds += seconds\n\ntimer = Timer('practice')\ntimer.add(25)\nprint(timer.label, timer.seconds)", 'practice' => 'Create a Book class with title and author, then add a method that returns a readable label.'],
                    ['title' => '12. Comprehensions, iterators and generators', 'explanation' => 'A comprehension builds a collection from an iterable in a compact, readable expression. An iterator yields one item at a time; a generator function uses yield to produce values lazily, which can save memory for large or streaming inputs. Prefer a regular loop if the comprehension becomes hard to understand.', 'example' => "squares = [number * number for number in range(1, 6)]\n\ndef read_nonblank(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        cleaned = line.strip()\n        if cleaned:\n            yield cleaned\n\nprint(squares)", 'practice' => 'Generate even numbers from a range, then write a generator that yields only non-empty lines.'],
                    ['title' => '13. Type hints, dataclasses and maintainable APIs', 'explanation' => 'Type hints document expected inputs and outputs and help editors and static checkers find mistakes; Python does not enforce them at runtime by default. Dataclasses remove repetitive initialization code for simple data records. Add hints where they clarify a public function or a complex value.', 'example' => "from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Lesson:\n    title: str\n    minutes: int\n\ndef total_minutes(lessons: list[Lesson]) -> int:\n    return sum(lesson.minutes for lesson in lessons)", 'practice' => 'Add type hints to your task-list functions and represent each task with a dataclass.'],
                    ['title' => '14. Decorators, context managers and asynchronous I/O', 'explanation' => 'A decorator wraps a function to add cross-cutting behavior such as logging; functools.wraps preserves its metadata. A context manager sets up and reliably releases a resource. async and await coordinate I/O-bound work without blocking the event loop; they do not automatically make CPU-heavy code faster.', 'example' => "from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef managed_message(label):\n    print(f'Open {label}')\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        print(f'Close {label}')\n\nwith managed_message('report'):\n    print('Working')", 'practice' => 'Wrap a function with a simple timing or logging decorator, then compare sequential and asynchronous network calls.'],
                    ['title' => '15. Testing, debugging and packaging a small app', 'explanation' => 'Tests turn expected behavior into repeatable checks. Start with small unit tests for edge cases, use assertions sparingly in application code, and run tests before sharing changes. Add a README with setup and run steps; keep secrets out of source control and pin production dependencies with a deliberate update process.', 'example' => "def is_even(value):\n    return value % 2 == 0\n\ndef test_is_even():\n    assert is_even(4) is True\n    assert is_even(5) is False\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    test_is_even()\n    print('All checks passed')", 'practice' => 'Build a command-line expense tracker with input validation, JSON storage, at least five tests and a README.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Can I learn Python with no coding background?', 'a' => 'Yes. Begin with variables, conditions and loops, and run every example yourself before moving to the next module.'],
                    ['q' => 'Do I need Python 3.15 for every project?', 'a' => 'The guide is checked against Python 3.15.0. Existing production projects may use a different supported version, so check the project and package compatibility before upgrading.'],
                ],
            ],
            'html' => [
                'name' => 'HTML', 'category' => 'Web markup', 'version' => 'HTML Living Standard',
                'versionNote' => 'HTML is maintained as a continuously updated Living Standard, not as numbered HTML 5.x releases. This guide follows the WHATWG standard checked on 21 October 2026.',
                'level' => 'Beginner to advanced', 'duration' => '10 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-filetype-html',
                'summary' => 'Build a web page from the beginning, then learn headings, links, images, forms, accessibility and page metadata using clear HTML examples.',
                'prerequisites' => 'No coding experience required. A text editor and modern browser are enough to create and inspect your first HTML file.',
                'outcome' => 'Write semantic, accessible documents and connect their structure cleanly to CSS and JavaScript.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Web & Mobile Development',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. A valid HTML document', 'explanation' => 'HTML is the structure and meaning of a web page; think of it as the frame and room plan of a house. The doctype tells the browser to use modern standards. The html element contains the whole page, head stores information about the page, and body holds the content people see. The charset setting lets the browser display letters and symbols correctly, including text in different languages.', 'example' => "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>My first page</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Hello, web</h1>\n</body>\n</html>", 'practice' => 'Create index.html with a title, one heading and one short paragraph; open it in your browser.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Elements, attributes and nesting', 'explanation' => 'An element usually has an opening tag, content and a closing tag. Attributes add information in the opening tag, such as href on a link. Nest elements in the correct order and quote attribute values; some elements, such as img and input, are void elements with no closing tag.', 'example' => "<p>Read the <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/\">reference guide</a>.</p>\n<img src=\"/images/keyboard.webp\" alt=\"Compact keyboard on a desk\">", 'practice' => 'Add a link inside a paragraph and an image with useful alternative text.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Headings, paragraphs, lists and page outline', 'explanation' => 'Headings give a page an outline, like the chapter titles in a book. Use one main h1 to say what the page is about, then use h2 and h3 for the sections inside it. Pick a heading because of its meaning, not because you want a certain font size; CSS controls appearance. Paragraphs are for sentences, while ordered and unordered lists make steps and groups easier to scan. This structure also helps screen-reader users move between sections.', 'example' => "<main>\n  <h1>Build a portfolio</h1>\n  <section aria-labelledby=\"steps-title\">\n    <h2 id=\"steps-title\">First steps</h2>\n    <ol><li>Choose a project</li><li>Write a plan</li></ol>\n  </section>\n</main>", 'practice' => 'Outline a learning article with one h1, three h2 sections and an ordered checklist.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Semantic layout landmarks', 'explanation' => 'Elements such as header, nav, main, article, section and footer communicate each region’s purpose. Use main once for the page’s primary content and nav for a significant group of navigation links. A div is still useful when no semantic element describes the group.', 'example' => "<header><a href=\"/\">Learning Notes</a></header>\n<nav aria-label=\"Primary\"><a href=\"/guides\">Guides</a></nav>\n<main><article><h1>Semantic HTML</h1><p>Meaning improves navigation.</p></article></main>\n<footer>Updated 2026</footer>", 'practice' => 'Refactor a page made of divs into landmarks without changing its visible content.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Links, paths and navigation', 'explanation' => 'An anchor creates navigation when it has an href. Use descriptive link text that makes sense out of context, prefer stable relative paths for pages in the same site, and reserve buttons for actions. Add a fragment identifier to link directly to a section with a matching id.', 'example' => "<nav aria-label=\"Guide chapters\">\n  <a href=\"#forms\">Forms and validation</a>\n</nav>\n<section id=\"forms\"><h2>Forms</h2></section>\n<a href=\"/learn/python\">Python learning guide</a>", 'practice' => 'Build a table of contents with three working in-page links.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Images, audio, video and responsive media', 'explanation' => 'Use img with meaningful alt text when an image communicates information; use alt="" for purely decorative images. Include width and height to reserve layout space, and offer captions or transcripts when media has spoken content. Use picture and srcset when you need responsive image sources.', 'example' => "<figure>\n  <img src=\"/images/chart.webp\" width=\"800\" height=\"450\"\n       alt=\"Monthly sign-ups rise from January to June\" loading=\"lazy\">\n  <figcaption>Practice project: monthly sign-ups.</figcaption>\n</figure>", 'practice' => 'Add a responsive image and write alt text that describes the information, not the file name.'],
                    ['title' => '7. Tables for real tabular data', 'explanation' => 'Use a table only when data has meaningful rows and columns, not to position page content. Add a caption and header cells so the relationships remain clear to screen readers. Keep complex tables small enough to understand and provide a simpler summary when needed.', 'example' => "<table>\n  <caption>Study plan</caption>\n  <thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Week</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">1</th><td>HTML basics</td></tr></tbody>\n</table>", 'practice' => 'Represent a three-week learning plan as a table with a caption and correctly scoped headers.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Forms, labels and native validation', 'explanation' => 'A label names a form control; connect it with for and the control’s id, or wrap the control. Choose the correct input type for useful browser behavior. Use required, min, max and pattern for simple validation, then validate again on the server because browser checks can be bypassed.', 'example' => "<form action=\"/signup\" method=\"post\">\n  <label for=\"email\">Email address</label>\n  <input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required autocomplete=\"email\">\n  <button type=\"submit\">Join</button>\n</form>", 'practice' => 'Create a sign-up form with a labelled email field, consent checkbox and submit button.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Accessible interaction and built-in elements', 'explanation' => 'Prefer native controls because browsers already provide keyboard behavior and accessibility semantics. Use button for an action and anchor for navigation. Add ARIA only when native HTML cannot express the needed role or state, and make every interaction usable with a keyboard.', 'example' => "<details>\n  <summary>What does semantic mean?</summary>\n  <p>It means the element communicates the purpose of its content.</p>\n</details>\n<button type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Show options</button>", 'practice' => 'Try your page using only Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter and Space; fix any control you cannot reach or operate.'],
                    ['title' => '10. Metadata, SEO and safe embedding', 'explanation' => 'A useful title and specific description summarize the page in search and browser tabs. Set canonical URLs and social preview metadata for published pages that need them, and keep headings aligned with the actual content. Embeds should use the narrowest permissions necessary; do not paste untrusted HTML into a page.', 'example' => "<head>\n  <title>HTML Forms: Labels, Types and Validation</title>\n  <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Learn accessible HTML forms with labelled inputs and native validation.\">\n  <link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://example.com/learn/html\">\n</head>", 'practice' => 'Give your practice article a unique title, accurate description and stable canonical URL.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Is HTML a programming language?', 'a' => 'HTML is a markup language: it describes document structure. CSS controls presentation and JavaScript adds behavior.'],
                    ['q' => 'Should I learn HTML before CSS?', 'a' => 'Yes. Learn semantic structure and labels first so you can style real content and build accessible interfaces.'],
                ],
            ],
            'css' => [
                'name' => 'CSS', 'category' => 'Style sheet language', 'version' => 'CSS Snapshot 2026',
                'versionNote' => 'CSS has independently versioned modules rather than one single version number. This guide uses the W3C CSS Snapshot 2026 as a standards map and favors widely supported features.',
                'level' => 'Beginner to advanced', 'duration' => '10 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-palette2',
                'summary' => 'Start with CSS selectors and spacing, then style readable pages and create responsive layouts with Flexbox, Grid, media queries and reusable colors.',
                'prerequisites' => 'Basic HTML helps, but each example is small enough to practise in a new HTML file.',
                'outcome' => 'Build responsive, accessible page layouts and debug style conflicts with a repeatable method.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Web & Mobile Development',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. Selectors and the cascade', 'explanation' => 'A selector tells CSS which HTML elements to style. The declarations inside the rule set things such as color, size and spacing. Sometimes several rules match the same element; the browser follows the cascade to decide which one wins. Simple selectors and clear component names make that decision easier to predict. If a style does not apply, inspect the matching rules before adding more specific selectors.', 'example' => "/* style.css */\nbody { color: #182538; }\n.lesson-card { padding: 1.25rem; }\n.lesson-card h2 { margin-block: 0 0.5rem; }", 'practice' => 'Style a heading and card with classes. Inspect the computed styles and identify which rule wins.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Box model, sizing and spacing', 'explanation' => 'CSS lays each element out as a box. The content is in the middle, padding is the space inside the edge, border is the edge itself, and margin is space outside it. With box-sizing: border-box, a card set to 400 pixels wide stays 400 pixels wide even after padding and a border are added. Logical properties such as margin-inline add space at the start and end, which also works for languages written right to left.', 'example' => "*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }\n.card { width: min(100%, 32rem); padding: 1.5rem; border: 1px solid #d5dce5; margin-inline: auto; }", 'practice' => 'Give a card a maximum width and center it; compare its width before and after border-box.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Color, typography and readable units', 'explanation' => 'Use rem for text and spacing that should scale with the root font size, and percentages or flexible units for fluid layouts. Choose a readable line length and contrast. CSS custom properties let a design system share colors and spacing consistently.', 'example' => ":root { --ink: #182538; --accent: #c5f467; }\nbody { color: var(--ink); font: 1rem/1.65 system-ui, sans-serif; }\n.article { max-width: 68ch; margin-inline: auto; }", 'practice' => 'Set up a small theme with three custom properties and check that body text remains legible at 200% zoom.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Normal flow, display and positioning', 'explanation' => 'Normal flow lays out block and inline content in document order. display changes how an element participates in layout. Positioning removes or offsets boxes in specific ways; sticky and fixed elements must not hide focused controls or important content.', 'example' => ".toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1rem; }\n.notice { position: sticky; inset-block-start: 0; z-index: 2; background: white; }", 'practice' => 'Make a heading-and-actions row with Flexbox; test the sticky banner at a narrow viewport.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Flexbox for one-dimensional layout', 'explanation' => 'Flexbox is made for arranging items in one direction: across a row or down a column. Imagine placing a heading and buttons on a shelf. justify-content controls their spacing along the shelf, align-items lines them up across it, and gap adds even space between items. When the screen gets too narrow, flex-wrap lets items move onto a new line instead of being squeezed or cut off.', 'example' => ".actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; }\n.actions > a { padding: 0.65rem 1rem; }", 'practice' => 'Create a row of action links that wraps neatly on a phone without using floats.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Grid for two-dimensional layout', 'explanation' => 'CSS Grid arranges items in rows and columns at the same time, so it works well for a gallery or a set of course cards. repeat() describes how many columns to make, while minmax() gives each column a safe minimum and a flexible maximum. auto-fit allows the browser to fit as many columns as the available space can hold. The HTML reading order stays important even if CSS moves items around visually.', 'example' => ".cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }", 'practice' => 'Make a responsive card grid that has one column on a phone and expands naturally on wider screens.'],
                    ['title' => '7. Responsive design and media queries', 'explanation' => 'Start with a layout that works in a narrow viewport, then add breakpoints only when the content needs them. Media queries can react to viewport width, user preferences such as reduced motion, and other environmental features. Avoid fixed page widths that cause horizontal scrolling.', 'example' => ".page { padding-inline: 1rem; }\n@media (min-width: 48rem) { .page { padding-inline: 2rem; } }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { scroll-behavior: auto; } }", 'practice' => 'Test your layout at 320px, 768px and 1280px, and correct any overflow or unreadable line lengths.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Pseudo-classes, pseudo-elements and states', 'explanation' => 'Pseudo-classes select an element’s state or relationship, such as :hover, :focus-visible and :checked. Pseudo-elements style a part of an element, such as ::before. Make focus indicators visible and ensure hover is never the only way to reveal key information.', 'example' => ".button:hover { filter: brightness(0.95); }\n.button:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #246b56; outline-offset: 3px; }\nli + li { margin-block-start: 0.5rem; }", 'practice' => 'Tab through your page and add a clear focus indicator to every interactive element.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Custom properties, layers and maintainable CSS', 'explanation' => 'Custom properties hold reusable values and can vary in the cascade. Cascade layers let you define a predictable order for broad styles, components and overrides. Organize rules around components and remove rules that no longer describe the interface.', 'example' => "@layer reset, base, components, utilities;\n@layer base { :root { --space: 1rem; } }\n@layer components { .panel { padding: var(--space); border-radius: 0.75rem; } }", 'practice' => 'Create a small layered stylesheet and change one custom property to restyle every panel.'],
                    ['title' => '10. Transitions, transforms and animation', 'explanation' => 'Transitions animate a property change and transforms move or scale an element without changing surrounding layout. Use animation to communicate state, not as decoration that blocks reading. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and avoid animating expensive properties when a transform or opacity will work.', 'example' => ".card { transition: transform 160ms ease, box-shadow 160ms ease; }\n.card:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .card { transition: none; } }", 'practice' => 'Add a subtle card interaction and verify that it is disabled for a reduced-motion preference.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Does CSS have a single latest version?', 'a' => 'No. CSS standards are developed module by module. The W3C CSS Snapshot collects the stable definition for a given year.'],
                    ['q' => 'Should I use Grid or Flexbox?', 'a' => 'Use Flexbox when arranging items along one axis; use Grid when rows and columns work together. They can also be combined.'],
                ],
            ],
            'scss' => [
                'name' => 'Sass / SCSS', 'category' => 'CSS preprocessor', 'version' => 'Dart Sass 1.105.2 · SCSS syntax',
                'versionNote' => 'Current Dart Sass release shown by the official CLI documentation when checked on 21 October 2026. SCSS is Sass syntax; compile it to standard CSS for browsers.',
                'level' => 'CSS foundations recommended', 'duration' => '9 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-filetype-scss',
                'summary' => 'Learn Sass and SCSS step by step: store design values, reuse style patterns, split code into modules, and compile your work into browser-ready CSS.',
                'prerequisites' => 'Learn CSS selectors, the box model and responsive rules first. SCSS extends CSS authoring; it does not replace understanding CSS.',
                'outcome' => 'Structure a small design system with reusable Sass modules and produce readable, browser-ready CSS.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Web & Mobile Development',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. What Sass and SCSS are', 'explanation' => 'Sass adds features that help you organize stylesheets, then compiles them into regular CSS. SCSS is the Sass syntax that looks most like the CSS you already know, so ordinary CSS rules can usually go straight into an .scss file. Browsers do not read SCSS directly; a compiler such as Dart Sass turns it into a .css file the browser can load. Think of SCSS as the source recipe and CSS as the finished dish.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
$brand: #245f4a;

.button {
  background: $brand;
  color: white;
}
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Install Dart Sass in a project, create styles.scss and compile it into styles.css.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Variables and useful values', 'explanation' => 'Sass variables store compile-time values and are useful for values you configure before building CSS. CSS custom properties remain available in the browser and can change at runtime. Use the right tool for the job: a Sass map for build-time token sets, a custom property for runtime theming.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
$space: 1rem;
$brand: #245f4a;

:root { --brand: #{$brand}; }
.card { padding: $space; color: var(--brand); }
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Create spacing and color variables, compile the file and inspect the resulting CSS.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Nesting and the parent selector', 'explanation' => 'Nesting places related rules together, and & refers to the current parent selector for states or modifiers. Keep nesting shallow: deeply nested rules generate long selectors that are harder to override. Nest related selectors only when it improves readability.', 'example' => ".button {\n  padding: 0.75rem 1rem;\n  &:hover { filter: brightness(0.95); }\n  &--quiet { background: transparent; }\n}", 'practice' => 'Convert a small button component to SCSS and keep the generated selectors short.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Partials and the modern module system', 'explanation' => 'Split large stylesheets into small files named with a leading underscore; these partials are not emitted as standalone CSS. Load and share public Sass APIs with @use and @forward. The older Sass @import rule is deprecated; move code toward modules instead of starting new projects with imports.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
// _tokens.scss
$brand: #245f4a;

// styles.scss
@use 'tokens';
.page-title { color: tokens.$brand; }
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Move tokens to a partial and access them through its namespace using @use.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Mixins for reusable declaration patterns', 'explanation' => 'A mixin inserts a reusable block of CSS declarations and can accept parameters or content. Use it when several components share a genuine pattern, not merely to give every rule a new name. Check the compiled output to avoid duplicating large blocks unnecessarily.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
@mixin focus-ring($color) {
  &:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid $color; outline-offset: 3px; }
}

.button { @include focus-ring(#245f4a); }
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Write one focus-ring mixin and apply it to a link and a button.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Functions, maps and token lookup', 'explanation' => 'Functions return a value for use in another expression; maps group related key-value settings. Prefer Sass’s built-in module functions through @use "sass:map" and @use "sass:color" instead of deprecated global helpers. Validate that a requested map key exists so mistakes fail clearly.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
@use 'sass:map';
$colors: ('brand': #245f4a, 'ink': #182538);

$brand: map.get($colors, 'brand');
.badge { color: $brand; }
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Create a color map and use map.get to style two components.'],
                    ['title' => '7. Loops and generated utilities', 'explanation' => 'Control directives such as @each, @for and @if generate CSS based on data. They can help produce a family of utilities, but broad generation can bloat stylesheets. Generate only classes your design system needs and inspect the output before shipping.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
$spaces: (1: .25rem, 2: .5rem, 3: 1rem);

@each $key, $value in $spaces {
  .gap-#{$key} { gap: $value; }
}
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Generate three spacing utility classes from a Sass map and review the emitted CSS.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Responsive components and CSS custom properties', 'explanation' => 'Use Sass to organize source and CSS custom properties when a value should respond to runtime themes or component state. Media queries can be written in nested component context; keep breakpoints consistent and consider the content rather than specific devices.', 'example' => <<<'CODE'
$card-padding: 1rem;
.card {
  padding: $card-padding;
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { padding: $card-padding * 1.5; }
}
CODE
, 'practice' => 'Create a card that increases its padding at one content-driven breakpoint and compare the CSS output.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Compile, debug and ship maintainable SCSS', 'explanation' => 'Run Dart Sass in watch mode while authoring and use source maps during development when available. Compile in CI or the build pipeline so deployed pages receive CSS. Treat compiler warnings as migration signals, and do not ship Sass source maps if they expose private source or paths.', 'example' => "# One-time compilation\nsass src/scss:public/css\n\n# Recompile when source files change\nsass --watch src/scss:public/css", 'practice' => 'Organize a small interface into tokens, base, components and utilities; compile it and check every stylesheet loads in the browser.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Are Sass and SCSS different languages?', 'a' => 'SCSS is one syntax supported by Sass. The indented .sass syntax is the other; both compile to CSS.'],
                    ['q' => 'Can a browser read SCSS directly?', 'a' => 'No. Compile SCSS to CSS before serving it to the browser.'],
                ],
            ],
            'bootstrap' => [
                'name' => 'Bootstrap', 'category' => 'CSS framework', 'version' => 'Bootstrap 5.3.8',
                'versionNote' => 'Latest stable 5.3 patch listed by the official version guide on 21 October 2026. Bootstrap 6 is an upcoming alpha; use it only for evaluation, not as the stable learning baseline.',
                'level' => 'HTML and CSS foundations recommended', 'duration' => '10 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-bootstrap',
                'summary' => 'Use Bootstrap to build responsive web pages with a grid, ready-made buttons, cards, forms and menus, then adjust the design for your project.',
                'prerequisites' => 'Know how HTML elements, classes and basic CSS work. Bootstrap makes common patterns faster; it does not remove those foundations.',
                'outcome' => 'Create responsive pages with reusable Bootstrap components, minimal JavaScript and project-specific styling.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Web & Mobile Development',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. Add Bootstrap to a page', 'explanation' => 'Bootstrap is a collection of ready-made CSS styles and optional JavaScript behaviors. Add its CSS file to use layout and component classes. Add the JavaScript bundle only if the page needs interactive features such as a dropdown or popup modal. Keep the CSS and JavaScript versions matched, and choose a specific version so your page does not silently change when a new release appears.', 'example' => "<link href=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.8/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n<script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.8/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js\" defer></script>", 'practice' => 'Create a page with the official CSS and add a styled heading, button and alert.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Containers, breakpoints and the grid', 'explanation' => 'Bootstrap’s grid helps you arrange page content into columns that can change size on different screens. A container sets a comfortable page width, a row groups columns, and column classes describe how much space each item needs. The 12-column idea is a measuring system, not a requirement to put 12 items on every page. In this example the article is wider than the related-links column on large screens, and they stack on small screens.', 'example' => "<div class=\"container py-4\">\n  <div class=\"row g-3\">\n    <main class=\"col-12 col-lg-8\">Article</main>\n    <aside class=\"col-12 col-lg-4\">Related links</aside>\n  </div>\n</div>", 'practice' => 'Build a two-column article layout that stacks on narrow screens.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Spacing, display and responsive utilities', 'explanation' => 'Utilities provide common one-purpose styles such as spacing, alignment and display. Responsive infixes apply a utility from a breakpoint upward. Use a small set of consistent utilities and prefer a named component class when a page repeats the same complex pattern.', 'example' => "<section class=\"d-flex flex-column flex-md-row align-items-md-center gap-3 py-4\">\n  <h2 class=\"mb-0 me-md-auto\">Your learning plan</h2>\n  <a class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"/learn/python\">Open guide</a>\n</section>", 'practice' => 'Make an action row stack on mobile and align horizontally at a medium breakpoint.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Buttons, cards and alerts', 'explanation' => 'Components combine styling conventions into predictable patterns. Use button variants for action hierarchy, card markup for grouped content and alerts for status messages. Don’t rely on color alone to communicate meaning; include clear text and choose appropriate semantics.', 'example' => "<article class=\"card h-100\">\n  <div class=\"card-body\">\n    <h2 class=\"h5 card-title\">HTML foundations</h2>\n    <p class=\"card-text\">Build a meaningful document structure.</p>\n    <a class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"/learn/html\">Start learning</a>\n  </div>\n</article>", 'practice' => 'Build a three-card learning path and make every card action clear and keyboard accessible.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Navigation bars and responsive navigation', 'explanation' => 'A navbar groups a brand, navigation links and responsive collapse behavior. The toggler must identify the controlled element and expose its expanded state. Use nav and list markup consistently and avoid putting every site action into one crowded menu.', 'example' => "<nav class=\"navbar navbar-expand-lg bg-body-tertiary\">\n  <div class=\"container\">\n    <a class=\"navbar-brand\" href=\"/\">Study Lab</a>\n    <button class=\"navbar-toggler\" type=\"button\" data-bs-toggle=\"collapse\" data-bs-target=\"#siteNav\" aria-controls=\"siteNav\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-label=\"Toggle navigation\"><span class=\"navbar-toggler-icon\"></span></button>\n    <div class=\"collapse navbar-collapse\" id=\"siteNav\"><div class=\"navbar-nav\"><a class=\"nav-link active\" href=\"/learn\">Learn</a></div></div>\n  </div>\n</nav>", 'practice' => 'Add a responsive navbar and verify the collapsed menu works with touch, mouse and keyboard.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Forms and validation feedback', 'explanation' => 'Bootstrap styles form controls but native HTML still defines their behavior and accessible labels. Use the right input type and associate labels with controls. Validation feedback should tell the user what happened and how to fix it; server-side validation is still required.', 'example' => "<div class=\"mb-3\">\n  <label for=\"email\" class=\"form-label\">Email address</label>\n  <input id=\"email\" class=\"form-control\" type=\"email\" required autocomplete=\"email\">\n  <div class=\"form-text\">Use an address you can access.</div>\n</div>", 'practice' => 'Create a two-field form with persistent labels and a visible validation message.'],
                    ['title' => '7. JavaScript plugins and data attributes', 'explanation' => 'Bootstrap’s JavaScript plugins add behaviors such as collapse, modal and dropdown. The bundle includes Popper where needed. Choose either data attributes or the documented JavaScript API, and avoid initializing the same plugin twice. Make sure interactive controls work without a mouse.', 'example' => "<button class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-bs-toggle=\"modal\" data-bs-target=\"#infoDialog\">Read details</button>\n<div class=\"modal fade\" id=\"infoDialog\" tabindex=\"-1\" aria-labelledby=\"infoTitle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n  <div class=\"modal-dialog\"><div class=\"modal-content\"><div class=\"modal-header\"><h2 class=\"modal-title fs-5\" id=\"infoTitle\">Course details</h2><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn-close\" data-bs-dismiss=\"modal\" aria-label=\"Close\"></button></div></div></div>\n</div>", 'practice' => 'Add a dialog that opens from a button, has a labelled heading and can be dismissed from the keyboard.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Theme and color modes', 'explanation' => 'Bootstrap 5.3 supports color modes that can be selected with data attributes and CSS variables. Make the chosen palette readable and ensure form controls, borders and focus indicators fit the mode. Persist a user choice only if your page provides a clear way to change it.', 'example' => "<html lang=\"en\" data-bs-theme=\"light\">\n<body>\n  <div class=\"card\"><div class=\"card-body\">Theme-aware content</div></div>\n</body>\n</html>", 'practice' => 'Switch a sample page between light and dark mode and check contrast and focus states in both.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Customize Bootstrap with Sass and CSS variables', 'explanation' => 'For small changes, override a CSS variable or add a project class after Bootstrap. For deep theme customization, compile Bootstrap Sass and set configuration before importing the framework. Keep custom rules scoped to your components so framework upgrades remain understandable.', 'example' => ":root {\n  --bs-primary: #245f4a;\n  --bs-primary-rgb: 36, 95, 74;\n}\n\n.btn-primary { --bs-btn-bg: #245f4a; --bs-btn-border-color: #245f4a; }", 'practice' => 'Customize primary button colors and test normal, hover, focus and disabled states.'],
                    ['title' => '10. Accessibility, performance and production checks', 'explanation' => 'Framework classes do not guarantee accessible content. Check landmarks, labels, focus order, contrast, responsive overflow and reduced-motion behavior. Include only the CSS and JavaScript features you use where the build system allows it, and test the final page in a real browser.', 'example' => "<a class=\"btn btn-outline-primary\" href=\"/learn/css\">\n  Continue to the CSS guide\n</a>\n<p class=\"visually-hidden\">This link opens the next lesson.</p>", 'practice' => 'Audit a mini landing page at keyboard-only, 200% zoom and phone width; fix every issue you can reproduce.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Is Bootstrap 6 ready for production?', 'a' => 'The official version guide lists Bootstrap 6 as upcoming alpha. This guide uses the stable Bootstrap 5.3 line.'],
                    ['q' => 'Do I need JavaScript to use Bootstrap?', 'a' => 'Core layout and visual components use CSS. Interactive components such as a collapsible navbar need Bootstrap JavaScript.'],
                ],
            ],
            'java' => [
                'name' => 'Java', 'category' => 'Programming language', 'version' => 'Java 27 · JDK 27',
                'versionNote' => 'Java 27 is the latest feature release as of 21 October 2026. Java 25 is the current LTS release; choose the LTS line when your project prioritizes long support and stable tooling.',
                'level' => 'Beginner to advanced', 'duration' => '12 modules · learn at your own pace', 'icon' => 'bi-cup-hot',
                'summary' => 'Start with your first Java program, then learn variables, methods, classes, collections, error handling and testing through practical code examples.',
                'prerequisites' => 'No prior programming experience is required. Install a JDK, which includes the compiler and tools needed to run Java programs.',
                'outcome' => 'Write structured Java programs, understand the runtime, use standard library collections, and test small applications.',
                'courseCategory' => 'Programming Foundations',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['title' => '1. JDK, compiler, JVM and first program', 'explanation' => 'The JDK is the toolkit you install to build Java programs. It includes javac, the compiler that turns your source code into bytecode. The JVM, or Java Virtual Machine, reads that bytecode and runs it on your computer. This separation is why compiled Java can run on different systems with a compatible JVM. Save the public class in a file with the same name, then run its main method to start the program.', 'example' => "class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, Java\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Save as Main.java, then run: java Main.java", 'practice' => 'Compile and run a program that prints your name and calculates 7 * 8.'],
                    ['title' => '2. Variables, primitive types and expressions', 'explanation' => 'Java asks you to tell it what kind of value each variable will hold, and the compiler checks that choice before the program runs. int stores whole numbers, double stores decimal numbers, boolean stores true or false, and String stores text. A variable with a clear name, such as lessons, is easier to read later. Watch out for integer division: 5 / 2 gives 2 because both values are whole numbers, so use a decimal type when you need 2.5.', 'example' => "int lessons = 4;\ndouble score = 87.5;\nboolean passed = score >= 40;\nString learner = \"Asha\";\nSystem.out.println(learner + \": \" + lessons + \", passed=\" + passed);", 'practice' => 'Calculate a bill total using a decimal amount and tax rate; print the result with a clear label.'],
                    ['title' => '3. Conditions, switch and loops', 'explanation' => 'A condition lets the program make a decision. if and else handle a yes-or-no choice, while switch is often easier to read when there are several named choices. Loops repeat work: for is useful when going through a range or list, and while repeats until a condition changes. Always check that a while loop can finish; otherwise the program may keep running forever.', 'example' => "int score = 72;\nString result = score >= 60 ? \"Pass\" : \"Retry\";\nfor (int number = 1; number <= 5; number++) {\n    System.out.println(number + \" → \" + result);\n}", 'practice' => 'Print a multiplication table with a loop and label each result.'],
                    ['title' => '4. Methods, parameters and return values', 'explanation' => 'A method is a named set of instructions that does one job, such as calculating tax. Parameters are the input values you give the method; the return value is the answer it gives back. Separating calculation from printing makes a method easier to reuse in a menu, a web page or a test. Keep each method focused, and give its inputs and result types clear names so its purpose is easy to see.', 'example' => "static double withTax(double subtotal, double rate) {\n    return subtotal * (1 + rate);\n}\n\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(withTax(100.0, 0.18));\n}", 'practice' => 'Write methods for the area of a rectangle and for checking whether an integer is even.'],
                    ['title' => '5. Arrays, strings and basic algorithms', 'explanation' => 'Arrays have a fixed length and store values of one type. String objects are immutable, so operations produce new strings. Use StringBuilder when repeatedly assembling text in a loop. Learn to trace an algorithm with a small input before optimizing it.', 'example' => "int[] scores = {72, 91, 84};\nint total = 0;\nfor (int score : scores) total += score;\ndouble average = (double) total / scores.length;\nSystem.out.println(\"Average: \" + average);", 'practice' => 'Find the minimum, maximum and average of a non-empty array of scores.'],
                    ['title' => '6. Classes, objects, constructors and encapsulation', 'explanation' => 'A class defines fields and methods; an object is an instance created from that class. Keep fields private and expose methods that protect valid state. A constructor establishes the object’s required values. Use this to distinguish a field from a parameter when names match.', 'example' => "final class BankAccount {\n    private final String owner;\n    private int balance;\n\n    BankAccount(String owner) { this.owner = owner; }\n    void deposit(int amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount must be positive\");\n        balance += amount;\n    }\n    int balance() { return balance; }\n}", 'practice' => 'Create a Book class that validates a non-empty title and exposes a readable description.'],
                    ['title' => '7. Interfaces, inheritance and composition', 'explanation' => 'An interface describes behavior that different classes can implement. Inheritance shares a subtype relationship but can couple child classes to parent behavior; composition lets a class use another object without claiming to be that type. Prefer interfaces and composition when they make dependencies easier to replace and test.', 'example' => "interface Notifier {\n    void send(String message);\n}\n\nfinal class ReminderService {\n    private final Notifier notifier;\n    ReminderService(Notifier notifier) { this.notifier = notifier; }\n    void remind() { notifier.send(\"Keep practising\"); }\n}", 'practice' => 'Implement a Notifier that prints to the console and inject it into ReminderService.'],
                    ['title' => '8. Collections and choosing the right data structure', 'explanation' => 'The Collections Framework provides lists, sets and maps for common data tasks. Use List when order and duplicates matter, Set for unique members, and Map for key-value lookup. Choose an implementation based on ordering and access needs, not habit; understand equality when using objects as set members or map keys.', 'example' => "import java.util.*;\n\nList<String> topics = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"types\", \"loops\"));\nSet<String> unique = new HashSet<>(topics);\nMap<String, Integer> minutes = new HashMap<>();\nminutes.put(\"types\", 25);\nSystem.out.println(unique + \" / \" + minutes.get(\"types\"));", 'practice' => 'Count words in a sentence with a Map and print each word with its frequency.'],
                    ['title' => '9. Generics, enums and records', 'explanation' => 'Generics let classes and methods work with typed values while preserving compile-time checks. An enum represents a fixed set of named options. A record is a concise data carrier whose components define its state; it is a good fit for simple immutable data, not every domain object.', 'example' => "enum Status { TODO, IN_PROGRESS, DONE }\n\nrecord Lesson(String title, int minutes) {}\n\nList<Lesson> plan = List.of(new Lesson(\"Collections\", 30));\nStatus status = Status.TODO;\nSystem.out.println(plan.getFirst().title() + \" - \" + status);", 'practice' => 'Represent a task with a record and use an enum for its status.'],
                    ['title' => '10. Exceptions, validation and resource safety', 'explanation' => 'Exceptions represent exceptional conditions and let a caller respond at the right boundary. Use checked exceptions when callers are expected to recover from an operation, and unchecked exceptions for invalid state or programmer errors. Use try-with-resources for closeable resources such as files and database connections.', 'example' => "try (var reader = java.nio.file.Files.newBufferedReader(java.nio.file.Path.of(\"notes.txt\"))) {\n    System.out.println(reader.readLine());\n} catch (java.io.IOException error) {\n    System.err.println(\"Could not read notes: \" + error.getMessage());\n}", 'practice' => 'Read a file safely and show a useful message if the path is missing.'],
                    ['title' => '11. Lambdas, streams and Optional', 'explanation' => 'A lambda provides an implementation of a functional interface. Streams describe a pipeline such as filter, map and collect; they are useful for transformations but can obscure side effects or simple loops. Optional models a value that may be absent in some APIs and should be handled explicitly.', 'example' => "List<String> longTopics = topics.stream()\n    .filter(topic -> topic.length() > 4)\n    .map(String::toUpperCase)\n    .toList();\nSystem.out.println(longTopics);", 'practice' => 'Filter a list of scores to passing values, map them to labels and calculate a summary.'],
                    ['title' => '12. Packages, testing, concurrency and next steps', 'explanation' => 'Packages group related types and help prevent name collisions. Build tools such as Maven or Gradle manage dependencies, compilation and test execution. Write tests around observable behavior. For concurrency, begin with executors and higher-level APIs; protect shared mutable state and avoid creating an unbounded thread per task.', 'example' => "import java.util.concurrent.*;\n\ntry (var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2)) {\n    Future<Integer> result = executor.submit(() -> 20 + 22);\n    System.out.println(result.get());\n} catch (InterruptedException error) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n} catch (ExecutionException error) {\n    throw new RuntimeException(error.getCause());\n}", 'practice' => 'Build a small console library tracker with packages, validation, a handful of unit tests and a README.'],
                ],
                'faq' => [
                    ['q' => 'Should beginners install Java 27 or Java 25?', 'a' => 'Java 27 is the latest feature release, while Java 25 is the current LTS release in this snapshot. Use the version expected by your course or employer; choose LTS when long support is the priority.'],
                    ['q' => 'Do I need to learn object-oriented programming first?', 'a' => 'Start with variables, conditions, loops and methods, then learn classes and interfaces. Small programs make those concepts easier to understand.'],
                ],
            ],
        ];

        return array_merge($guides, GitHubCICDGuide::all());
    }
